  "text": "A widow with one jar of oil out-earned her debt. Second Kings four, opening scene. A woman comes to Elisha crying, and her situation is this. Her husband is dead, he feared the Lord, and the creditor is coming to take her two sons to be bondmen. Not the furniture. The children. That is the debt system she is trapped in. Elisha asks her, what shall I do for thee? Tell me, what hast thou in the house? And she says, thine handmaid hath not any thing in the house, save a pot of oil. One pot. That is the whole inventory. And he tells her, go borrow thee vessels abroad of all thy neighbours, even empty vessels. Borrow not a few. Then shut the door on yourself and your sons, and start pouring. So she does. And the oil keeps coming. Vessel after vessel, until she says to her son, bring me yet a vessel, and he says, there is not a vessel more. And the oil stayed. She sells it, pays the debt, and lives on the rest. The supply stopped exactly when the containers did.",
